#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120962} :activity/id #uuid "796e396f-0c48-5eaf-a61f-e8b2d3339d65" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966 :topic/id #uuid "76391ee0-ed5a-5ed3-8cef-c09827219e58" :topic/math-academy-id 1765} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121089 :tutorial/content "Given a sphere with radius\n\n$$\nr=4\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nhow do we calculate its surface area?\n\n![](images/bd/bd7da8538c268a1dd9e17dc8f4185ba3db5847dca21d1eb408d0af2683ba64d4.png)\n\nWe can calculate the surface area $S$ of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=4\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi (4)^2 = 64\\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nWe often express the surface area of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi$. We can also get a numerical approximation using a calculator. In this case, we have\n\n$$\nS \\approx 201.06 \\:\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "1896920b-682d-58fe-874e-43fed82f00a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7fdd147a-47dd-5a6f-b15f-9c57770ebdeb" :step/math-academy-id 69030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119971 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119719 :question/id #uuid "fba82316-1ea7-5c90-88d7-37b96172a7f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9004"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91583cc4-29ef-5824-baee-f101cbf49be9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120117}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Sphere Given Its Radius or Diameter"} :step/id #uuid "07b710f9-6fc0-50e2-b886-f84e20d664e2" :step/math-academy-id 69031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120964}} {:db/id